{
  "concept": "The recursion tree for T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn expands stage by stage into lg n + 1 levels that each cost exactly cn, so the total merge-sort cost is cn lg n + cn = Θ(n lg n).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a): the single term T(n) printed alone, with panel label '(a)' beneath",
    "Panel (b): root node 'cn' with two straight edges descending to leaf terms 'T(n/2)' and 'T(n/2)', panel label '(b)'",
    "Panel (c): root 'cn' branching to two 'cn/2' nodes, each branching to two 'T(n/4)' nodes (four total), panel label '(c)'",
    "Panel (d): fully expanded tree — root 'cn'; level 1 two 'cn/2' nodes; level 2 four 'cn/4' nodes; short dashed edges descending from each cn/4 node indicating omitted levels; vertical dotted continuation lines",
    "Bottom leaf row in (d): repeated constant 'c' labels with a horizontal ellipsis '···' in the middle",
    "Curly brace under the leaf row labeled 'n' (number of leaves)",
    "Double-headed vertical arrow at far left spanning the full tree height, labeled 'lg n'",
    "Dotted horizontal arrows from each drawn level to the right-hand cost column",
    "Right-hand level-sum column: 'cn' opposite each level, a vertical ellipsis ':' for the omitted levels, and 'cn' opposite the leaf row",
    "Horizontal rule beneath the right column and the summary text 'Total: cn lg n + cn'",
    "Panel label '(d)' centered under the large tree",
    "Recurrence caption T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn shown as the governing equation",
    "Color coding: unresolved T(...) terms in a distinct hue (e.g. gray/blue), resolved cost nodes cn, cn/2, cn/4, c in black; highlighted level row in accent color when overlay is on"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "expandStage",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Expand recursion",
      "states": ["T(n)", "one split", "two splits", "full tree"],
      "default": "T(n)",
      "teaches": "Each unresolved T term is replaced by its own cost node plus two subproblems, so the tree is literally the recurrence applied over and over.",
      "...": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "problemSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Problem size n (powers of 2)",
      "range": [4, 1024, 1],
      "default": 16,
      "teaches": "Doubling n adds exactly one level to the tree while doubling the leaves, so height is lg n and the total grows as n lg n, not n².",
      "...": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "levelCosts",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Level-cost overlay",
      "options": ["off", "on"],
      "default": "off",
      "teaches": "Every level, no matter how many nodes it has, sums to the same cn — because node count doubles exactly as node cost halves.",
      "...": ""
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start with T(n)",
      "narration": "The whole cost of sorting n elements is written as the single unknown T(n) — panel (a). Nothing is resolved yet.",
      "state": { "expandStage": "T(n)", "problemSize": 16, "levelCosts": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Apply the recurrence once",
      "narration": "T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn: the cn merge cost becomes a real node at the root, and two half-size unknowns T(n/2) hang below it.",
      "state": { "expandStage": "one split", "problemSize": 16, "levelCosts": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Expand again",
      "narration": "Each T(n/2) is replaced the same way, producing two cn/2 cost nodes and four T(n/4) subproblems. The rule is identical at every level.",
      "state": { "expandStage": "two splits", "problemSize": 16, "levelCosts": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Recurse to the leaves",
      "narration": "Continuing until subproblems have size 1 gives n leaves each costing c, and a tree of height lg n marked by the arrow on the left.",
      "state": { "expandStage": "full tree", "problemSize": 16, "levelCosts": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Add up each level",
      "narration": "Turn on the level sums: level 0 is cn, level 1 is 2 · cn/2 = cn, level 2 is 4 · cn/4 = cn — every row costs exactly cn.",
      "state": { "expandStage": "full tree", "problemSize": 16, "levelCosts": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change n and watch the height",
      "narration": "Raise n to 64: the leaf count grows but the number of cn rows only rises to lg n + 1. The total is cn lg n + cn = Θ(n lg n).",
      "state": { "expandStage": "full tree", "problemSize": 64, "levelCosts": "on" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Node cost halves at exactly the rate node count doubles, so every level costs the same cn — which means the only thing left to count is the number of levels, lg n + 1.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Top third: three small panels (a), (b), (c) left-to-right showing progressive expansion, panel letters centered below each. Bottom two-thirds: the large tree of panel (d), root centered near the top, binary branching with straight thin black edges, node labels in italic serif math (cn, cn/2, cn/4, c). Dashed short edges below the cn/4 row and vertical dotted lines down to the leaf row suggest omitted levels. Leaf row is a line of 'c' labels with a '···' in the middle, under a wide curly brace labeled n. Far left: tall double-headed arrow labeled 'lg n'. Right side: a column aligned with each drawn level, connected by dotted horizontal arrows, every entry reading cn, with a vertical ellipsis between the cn/4 row and the leaf row; a horizontal rule under the column and 'Total: cn lg n + cn' beneath it. Use black for resolved cost nodes; render unresolved T(·) terms in a muted blue/gray so the expansion button visibly converts blue T terms into black cost nodes plus new blue children. When the level-cost overlay is on, tint the active row band a warm accent and make the dotted arrow and its cn label bold; the Total line fades in only after all rows are shown. Keep everything on a white background with thin black strokes, textbook style.",
  "$schema_note": ""
}

